---
import ArrowUpRight from '../components/ArrowUpRight.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import { BOOKING_URL } from '../config';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbList, serviceNode } from '../lib/schema';
import { ENTITY_STATEMENT, SERVICE_AREA_PLACES, SITE_URL } from '../site';

const title = 'AI & Workflow Automation Consultant in Northern Virginia | Danny McGiffin';
const description =
	'Workflow automation and AI consulting for businesses in Herndon, Northern Virginia, and the Washington, DC area. Diagnose expensive manual work, redesign the process, then automate what should not require a person.';
const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/northern-virginia-ai-workflow-automation/`;

const structuredData = [
	breadcrumbList([
		{ name: 'Home', url: `${SITE_URL}/` },
		{ name: 'Northern Virginia consulting', url: pageUrl }
	]),
	serviceNode({
		id: `${pageUrl}#service`,
		name: 'AI & Workflow Automation Consulting',
		description,
		url: pageUrl,
		areaServed: SERVICE_AREA_PLACES
	})
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} structuredData={structuredData}>
	<SiteHeader />
	<main id="main-content">
		<header class="offer-hero section-dark">
			<div class="shell">
				<p class="eyebrow">Herndon, Virginia /</p>
				<h1 class="display">AI &amp; Workflow Automation<br />Consulting in<br /><em>Northern Virginia</em></h1>
				<p class="offer-deck">
					Understand the work. Improve the system. Automate what should not require human effort.
				</p>
			</div>
		</header>

		<article class="prose service-prose shell">
			<p>
				{ENTITY_STATEMENT} I help small and midsize businesses find expensive manual work, redesign the
				process around it, and then decide whether AI, automation, an integration, or a simpler operating
				change is the right fix.
			</p>
			<p>
				Most companies do not have an AI problem first. They have a work problem: too many handoffs, too
				much re-entry, too much delay, and too many capable people doing machine work. Software gets
				blamed. Headcount gets added. A tool gets purchased. The underlying process stays the same.
			</p>

			<h2>What I do</h2>
			<p>
				I work at the join between operations and software. I sit with the people who actually do the
				work, map how a process runs today, put numbers on the cost, and redesign the workflow before
				anyone picks a tool. Then I can build the automation, agents, or integrations the new process
				needs, put them into the real workflow, and measure whether they changed the business.
			</p>
			<p>
				The sequence matters. You cannot automate a process you have not understood. AI will not fix a
				broken handoff, a missing decision rule, or a system that cannot share the information the next
				person needs. Those are operating problems. Treat them as operating problems.
			</p>

			<h2>The problems this is for</h2>
			<p>Owners usually come to this work when something concrete is going wrong:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>A quote, intake, or approval sits in a queue while revenue waits.</li>
				<li>The same information is typed into two or three systems because nothing talks to anything else.</li>
				<li>A senior person is the process: if they are out, the work stops.</li>
				<li>Rework is normal. Files come back incomplete. Status lives in email.</li>
				<li>An AI initiative produced demos and meeting summaries, but no change in time, cost, or throughput.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Those are not vague transformation problems. They are expensive, repeatable, and measurable. That
				is the only kind of work I take on.
			</p>

			<h2>How the diagnostic works</h2>
			<p>
				I start with an <a href="/ai-opportunity-sprint/">AI Opportunity Sprint</a>: a fixed-price,
				two-week review of two to four workflows you choose with me. I interview the people who do the
				work, map how it actually runs, and cost it out — hours, handoffs, rework, and the delay it
				creates downstream. Nothing is purchased or installed during the review.
			</p>
			<p>
				The point of the diagnostic is not to produce a slide about AI. It is to find the work that costs
				too much and put a number on it. If there is no real opportunity, you get the money back and keep
				the report.
			</p>
			<p>What I am looking for is operational friction that has a business cost:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Work that is frequent, standardized, and currently done by a person because no one redesigned it.</li>
				<li>Handoffs that drop context, so the next person has to reconstruct the file.</li>
				<li>Judgment that is real, mixed in with gathering, classifying, drafting, and routing that is not.</li>
				<li>Tools that already exist but do not carry the work forward without a human acting as glue.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Redesign the work before you automate it</h2>
			<p>
				Once the current process is on paper, the next question is not “which tool?” It is whether this
				workflow should exist in this shape at all. Some steps should disappear. Some should be done
				earlier. Some should stop requiring a senior person. Some should stay human because they require
				judgment, a relationship, or a decision the business is not willing to automate.
			</p>
			<p>
				Only after that redesign do I look at the mechanism. Sometimes the answer is a clearer process and
				a better checklist. Sometimes it is an integration between systems you already pay for. Sometimes
				it is automation of a narrow, well-bounded task. Sometimes AI belongs in the workflow — drafting,
				classifying, extracting, watching a queue — with a person still responsible for the result.
			</p>
			<p>
				I will tell you which of those it is. I will not invent an AI project because that is what you
				asked for on the intake form.
			</p>

			<h2>Implementation and measurement</h2>
			<p>
				If we decide to build, I implement against the redesigned process, not against a feature list. The
				work goes into the actual workflow. The team is trained on how to use it. Then we measure the
				thing we said we would change: time, cost, throughput, error rate, or owner hours returned to the
				business.
			</p>
			<p>
				If it cannot be measured, it is not finished. A working demo is not a working system.
			</p>

			<h2>Who this is for</h2>
			<p>
				This is for an owner or operator of a small or midsize business who can point at a workflow and
				say it costs too much — in hours, delay, rework, or dependence on one person. You need to be
				willing to let me talk to the people who do the work and look at how it actually runs. I cannot
				diagnose a process I am not allowed to see.
			</p>
			<p>
				It is a good fit if you want the operating system improved, not a tool tour. You are open to the
				answer being “fix the process” rather than “buy AI.”
			</p>

			<h2>Who this is not for</h2>
			<p>This is not a fit if you want a chatbot on the website, a generic AI workshop, or a vendor to implement a tool you have already chosen without examining the work. It is also not a fit if you need a staff-augmentation team, a same-week software install, or a guarantee that AI is the answer before anyone has looked at the process.</p>
			<p>
				If you are looking for a technology partner first and an operating diagnosis second, I am the
				wrong person.
			</p>

			<h2>Based in Herndon, working across the region</h2>
			<p>
				I am based in Herndon, Virginia. I work with businesses across Northern Virginia and the
				Washington, DC area, including Reston, Chantilly, Fairfax, Tysons, McLean, Vienna, Ashburn,
				Leesburg, Arlington, Alexandria, Loudoun County, and Fairfax County. Some of the work is on site.
				Some of it is remote. The location that matters is where the work happens, not where a slide deck
				is delivered.
			</p>
			<p>
				I do not have offices in each of those places, and I will not pretend to. Herndon is home base.
				The region is the working area.
			</p>
		</article>

		<section class="offer-page-cta section-dark" aria-labelledby="service-cta-title">
			<div class="shell">
				<p class="eyebrow">Start with the work/</p>
				<h2 id="service-cta-title" class="display">If a workflow is costing you, <em>let’s look at it.</em></h2>
				<p class="offer-deck">
					Bring the process that is slow, expensive, or stuck on one person. We will decide whether it is
					worth a full review.
				</p>
				<div class="closing-actions">
					<a class="button button--primary" href={BOOKING_URL}>Book a call <ArrowUpRight /></a>
					<a class="text-link" href="/ai-opportunity-sprint/">AI Opportunity Sprint <ArrowUpRight /></a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
